  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe initial phase of our mid to late week rain episode is in \nprogress across Lower Mi this afternoon which continues in a similar \nfashion tonight. Expectations for resident dry air in the low levels \nas a limiting factor proved effective during the morning followed by \nsome gains in top down saturation this afternoon. Similar conditions \ncontinue tonight as the initial mid level moisture transport and \nsmall scale/low amplitude short wave forcing today shifts toward a \ndeeper and stronger low level jet driven configuration tonight. This \noccurs under further surface frontal development spanning the Plains \nstates connected to the broad northern stream trough and 4 Corners \nsystem. Low level jet forcing increasingly taps into the \ntropical/Gulf hybrid moisture plume to fuel elevated instability and \nscattered to numerous coverage of lower QPF showers tonight and \nThursday morning.  \n\nA transition toward the more intense phase of this event occurs \nThursday afternoon leading to a peak Thursday night, beginning with \nthe surface to mid level theta-e ridge building over Lower Mi in a \nfavorable position along and ahead of the Midwest cold front. \nForcing along and immediately post front is projected to increase \nconsiderably due to a constructive combination of the inbound \nnorthern stream mid level trough and entrance region of the upper \njet. The resulting increased QPF response in model solutions looks \ngood during this time as the stronger forcing combines with hybrid \ntropical resulting in PW near 2 inches. QPF among the 30/12Z \ndeterministic models produce 6 hr totals around 1 inch in good \nagreement with HREF and REFS PMMs over central/SE Mi Thursday night. \nFlooding potential is mitigated slightly by the quick frontal \npassage (see Hydrology Discussion below) that sweeps showers out of \nthe area Friday morning. \n\nSurface high pressure spans the northern Plains and Midwest by \nFriday morning which builds aggressively into the Great Lakes during \nthe day. It brings cooler and less humid air back into the region by \nevening highlighted by guidance low temperatures projected to reach \nthe mid and upper 30s by Saturday morning. Rapid progression of \nsystems bring another low and cold front through the Great Lakes \nSunday. For now, this is a dry frontal passage for SE Mi followed by \nreinforcing high pressure for early next week.",
